---
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# command.md.example
# This is the template for a slash command. Commands are short, focused
# slash-command bodies invoked via /<name> in tools that support them
# (Claude Code, VS Code Copilot's prompt files).
#
# Copy to commands/<your-command-name>.md (NOT inside a folder — commands
# are flat files), edit, then run `make register`.
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

name: example-command
description: One-line description of what this command does.
author: your-name

presets:
  - quality-gates

# Optional allowlist. Many tools don't support slash commands at all
# (Cursor, Antigravity, Gemini CLI). For tools that DO support them
# but only honour a subset of features, scope here.
tools:
  - claude-code
  - vscode-copilot
  - copilot-cli

# Optional per-tool overrides for prompt-file frontmatter.
overrides:
  vscode-copilot:
    # Copilot prompt-file frontmatter:
    #   mode: ask | edit | agent   (which Copilot chat mode the prompt runs in)
    #   tools: ['codebase', 'githubRepo']  (tool allowlist while running)
    #   model: claude-sonnet-4-6
    mode: agent
  copilot-cli:
    mode: agent
---

# /example-command

Open with a one-line summary of what running this command does.

## Usage

```
/example-command [optional-args]
```

For tools that support argument passing (Claude Code's `$ARGUMENTS`),
spell out what each argument means. For chat-only tools, write the
command body to handle natural-language input.

## What it does

A numbered procedure. Slash commands are most useful when they encode
a deterministic walk-through:

1. Read the staged or current diff.
2. Group changes by area.
3. Produce a description with title, summary, change list, test plan.

## Output shape

Show the kind of output the user should see. Commands that don't
pin their output shape drift; commands that do are pleasant to invoke
repeatedly because they're predictable.

## Style guidance

What house style to follow in the response. Pithy headings, no emoji,
no "I think", etc.

## Gotchas

- **Commands are FLAT files.** Unlike skills and agents, commands
  live at `commands/<name>.md`, not in a subfolder. The manifest
  generator picks files by name; subfolders are ignored.
- **Tools without slash commands silently skip.** Cursor doesn't have
  slash commands — installing this for Cursor warns and skips. For
  parity with those tools, consider exposing the same logic as a
  skill body instead.
- **Argument passing varies by tool.** Claude Code substitutes
  `$ARGUMENTS`; VS Code Copilot prompt files don't have a stable
  argument syntax (yet). Write the command to handle the trailing
  text in either form.
- **`mode: agent` vs `mode: edit`/`ask`.** For Copilot prompts the
  `mode` frontmatter governs which chat surface the prompt lands in.
  Default `agent` matches the toolkit's broader assumption that the
  consuming tool has an agent loop. Set `edit` for prompts that
  produce only a code diff with no chat back-and-forth.

